olding him, and showing concern (Frame 7, 9, 10–13, 19–23).\n\n---\n\n**3) Rescue/saving action occurring?**  \n✅ **Partially.** The orange brother is shown falling from a cliff (Frame 1–3), suggesting he was in danger or attempting a rescue. He lands in a cave near Grandpa (Frame 4–5), implying he reached him. However, **there is no clear visual of him actively “saving” Grandpa** — rather, he appears to have been injured or incapacitated *during* the attempt. Grandpa is the one who tends to him afterward.\n\n---\n\n**4) Immediate consequence affecting orange brother?**  \n✅ **Yes.** After landing, the orange brother is unconscious or severely weakened (Frame 4–5, 8, 14–15). He later awakens (Frame 16, 18) but appears distressed and is crying (Frame 17, 23). This suggests he suffered injury or trauma — but **not deafness**.\n\n---\n\n**5) Visual indication of deafness?**  \n❌ **No.** There is **no visual cue** indicating deafness. He does not cover his ears, show confusion at sounds, or react to auditory stimuli. In Frame 23, he is crying and covering his eyes/face — which suggests emotional pain or physical injury, not auditory impairment. No sound waves, ear icons, or “no sound” symbols appear. His eyes are shown reacting to Grandpa’s touch and voice (Frame 19–22), indicating he can hear.\n\n---\n\n**6) Interaction between orange brother and grandpa?**  \n✅ **Yes.** Grandpa is seen kneeling beside him (Frame 6), holding him (Frame 10–13), embracing him (Frame 19–22), and comforting him (Frame 23). The orange brother responds emotionally — opening his eyes, crying, and clinging to Grandpa — which implies **he is aware and responsive to Grandpa’s presence and voice**.\n\n---\n\n### Conclusion:\n\nWhile the orange calabash brother does suffer a consequence after reaching Grandpa (injury, unconsciousness, emotional distress), **there is no evidence in these frames that he becomes deaf**. The visual cues point to physical and emotional trauma, not auditory impairment.\